### 40–50 minutes: Build a reusable classifier

Let's turn the prompts into a reusable function.

```
def classify_request(message, use_examples=False):    instructions = """    Classify the portfolio request into exactly one category:    RESUME, PROJECT, or CERTIFICATE.    Return only the category name.    """    examples = ""    if use_examples:        examples = """        Request: Update my CV.        Category: RESUME        Request: Add my GitHub repository.        Category: PROJECT        Request: Upload my course certificate.        Category: CERTIFICATE        """    prompt = f"""    {instructions}    {examples}    Request: {message}    Category:    """    return ask_model(prompt)print(classify_request("Fix my resume education section."))print(classify_request(    "Add my RAG chatbot project.",    use_examples=True
```

Expected semantic categories:

```
RESUME
PROJECT
CERTIFICATE
```

These are expected results, not guaranteed exact model outputs.

### 50–55 minutes: Evaluate multiple requests

```
test_cases = [    ("Update my professional summary.", "RESUME"),    ("Add my GitHub machine learning project.", "PROJECT"),    ("Upload my internship completion certificate.", "CERTIFICATE"),    ("Correct the education details in my CV.", "RESUME"),    ("Add my chatbot demo link.", "PROJECT"),]correct = 0for message, expected in test_cases:    prediction = classify_request(        message,        use_examples=True    ).strip().upper()    is_correct = prediction == expected    if is_correct:        correct += 1    print("Request:", message)    print("Expected:", expected)    print("Predicted:", prediction)    print("Correct:", is_correct)    print("-" * 40)accuracy = correct / len(test_cases)print(f"Accuracy: {accuracy:.0%}")
```

Your result will depend on the model's actual predictions. With five examples, each correct prediction changes the accuracy by 20 percentage points.

For a more reliable evaluation, you would need more varied test cases.

